WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.735
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Vanessa Bayer.

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.938
So you went to QVC's studios
to research.

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:08.040
-Yes, yes.
-And the show sort of has

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.778
that on-camera personas,
off-camera personas,

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.713
a little bit of how people
interact with each other.

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:16.048
It does seem like a bit similar
to "SNL."

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:18.184
-Yes, there's definitely a lot
of similarities

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:19.953
because it's live TV
and everything.

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:22.454
And in the show, my character,
you know, shows up

00:00:22.454 --> 00:00:24.757
to her first day of work,
and she's so starstruck

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:27.726
by the other hosts that she's,
like, been -- you know, like,

00:00:27.726 --> 00:00:29.695
Molly Shannon's character
and all the people

00:00:29.695 --> 00:00:32.098
that she's been kind of watching
from home.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:35.401
And when I showed up at my first
day at "SNL," I was like,

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:37.136
"What the hell?"

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:40.640
Like, it was just like seeing
Andy Samberg and Abby Elliott

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.975
and, like, Kristen Wiig.

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:44.443
I was just, like, so starstruck.

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:46.545
And I had to act normal.

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:49.448
And it was so weird because
people are like -- you know,

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:51.984
like, "Where are you staying?"

00:00:51.984 --> 00:00:55.388
You know, and I was like,
"Yeah, um, like, at a hotel."

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:59.358
And they're like, "Yeah, like,
how's, you know --"

00:00:59.358 --> 00:01:01.361
What do people say
in conversation?

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:02.595
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:04.030
-I think everybody's hearing
this being like,

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.132
"I've had this exact
conversation."

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:07.967
-But, you know, they'd be like,
It's --

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:10.203
-It sounds like me, 'cause that
is how I talked back then.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.771
I'd be like, "Yeah, um,
where are you staying?"

00:01:12.771 --> 00:01:14.574
-That's me doing you.

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:15.942
-It was probably you were
talking to me.

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:18.978
-Yeah, they would just be like,
you know, and I'd just be

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:20.747
like, "Uh, yeah, I guess so."

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.048
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:24.184
-Were you starstruck when you
met the QVC people?

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:27.520
-Yes. Okay, so Jeremy Beiler,
who I created the show with,

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.755
he was a wonderful writer
at "SNL."

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:32.125
We went on this tour of QVC
together.

00:01:32.125 --> 00:01:37.029
And then they allowed us to meet
these two hosts that I watched

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:39.666
when I was a kid that are still
hosts there, Jane Treacy

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:42.535
and Mary Beth Roe.
They're the best hosts.

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:48.507
It sounds like a few of you --
and I got to meet them.

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:50.943
And even after seven years on
"SNL," where we would have

00:01:50.943 --> 00:01:52.678
different celebrities --
-Huge celebrities.

00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:54.747
-Huge celebrity hosts,
musical guests.

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:58.952
I was, like, so starstruck by
these two QVC hosts.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.487
And they did not disappoint.

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:03.489
They told -- we talked to them
for like an hour, and it went by

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:06.492
so quickly because they are so
incredible at talking.

00:02:06.492 --> 00:02:08.294
-Yeah.
-And the other thing

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:09.862
that I always think about is,
you know,

00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:12.965
on QVC, they take --
and any home shopping network,

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:14.400
they take callers.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.569
And, like, that's the one
element that we didn't have

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:18.571
at "SNL," was we didn't have
callers.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.406
-Like mid-sketch callers?
-Yeah.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:23.308
People being like,
"How do you think it's going?"

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:25.110
They would just be like, "Ugh,
the cast isn't as good

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.446
as when I was growing up."

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.181
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:30.316
-Also, in the show,
there's someone in

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:32.184
your ear being like,
"This isn't going well."

00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:34.720
-Yeah, yeah.
-It would be very bad if Lorne,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.690
mid-sketch, could be like,
"We're dying."

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.726
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.726 --> 00:02:43.495
Did you -- so, alright,
one of the -- not to

00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:48.001
give anything away, but you use
an element in the show of sort

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.969
of using your cancer --
-Yes, yeah.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:52.605
-To garner sympathy at a job.
-Yes, totally.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.807
-In real life, was this a thing?

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:58.277
-It's always been sort of a
thing where, like, you know,

00:02:58.277 --> 00:03:00.880
when I was younger, I would use
it to, like, get to go to school

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.615
late, and the attendance woman
would never question me.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:05.451
Like, I kind of used it for all
the perks because, you know,

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:08.520
like, I earned it.
And so I feel like --

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.490
but, yeah, I remember once
when I was at "SNL,"

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:15.027
I was flying with Lorne on,
like, his jet to L.A.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:20.299
or something, and he started
asking me about when I was sick.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:22.101
And I was like, "oh, my God."

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:24.304
I'm like -- this is, like, one
area where he's,

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.838
like, genuinely interested.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:29.342
Like I can -- I'm, like, at the
height of my, like, power here,

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:30.976
like, talking about when I was
sick.

00:03:30.976 --> 00:03:32.311
And he was asking me questions.

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:35.013
And I was, like, really, like,
you know, I'm kind of

00:03:35.013 --> 00:03:37.783
the belle of the ball --
the plane.

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:39.552
And so I was telling him all
about when I was sick.

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:40.820
And he was really interested.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.421
And I was like, he probably
thinks it's really cool.

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:43.556
Like, I made it through
this thing

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.057
and I have this experience.

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:48.527
And then I sort of finished
talking about it with him,

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:51.096
and there was, like,
this silence.

00:03:51.096 --> 00:03:56.936
So I just went, "Um, hey, Lorne,
what's John Travolta like?"

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:58.471
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:01.073
And immediately I went from
being, like, so cool

00:04:01.073 --> 00:04:03.142
to, like, such a little dummy.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.610
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:08.681
He was just like, "He's nice."
And I was like, "Cool, cool."

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:12.418
And then a week later, I came
back to work, and I saw

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:13.853
Kristen Wiig just randomly.

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.388
And I was like, oh -- she was
like, "How was your break?"

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:16.622
And I was like, "It was good.

00:04:16.622 --> 00:04:18.124
I was on a plane with Lorne
and I asked him,

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:19.425
what's John Travolta like?"

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:20.859
I'm like, "Isn't that
embarrassing?

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:21.994
I'm sure he doesn't even
remember."

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:23.362
And she was like, "Oh, yeah,
he told me."

00:04:23.362 --> 00:04:26.399
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:35.341
-Yeah, it was -- we were all on
a text thing with him

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.342
talking about it.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.877
[ Laughter ]

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:40.212
-He thinks you suck, yeah.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:43.782
-I do like -- in our final
minute here, any things

00:04:43.782 --> 00:04:47.987
from your "SNL" days that you
felt deserved a chance to be on

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:49.555
the show that didn't make it?

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:52.391
-Well, you know, Seth, there was
a sketch called "Boids."

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:54.593
-"Boids" -- B-O-I-D-S.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:56.595
-Yeah.
-That Taran and I wrote

00:04:56.595 --> 00:05:00.499
for Will Ferrell where we work
at, like, a zoo or a place

00:05:00.499 --> 00:05:03.869
where -- where there's, like,
a big bird display.

00:05:03.869 --> 00:05:05.871
-Yeah.
-And we play these guys

00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:08.875
who go like, "Hey, you want to
see the boids?"

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:12.345
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.480
-You pointed like that was going
better than --

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.248
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.248 --> 00:05:18.951
Oh, sorry.
We have a caller.

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.086
We have a caller.

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:21.287
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:24.123
-I'm trying to, like, will it
into being funny.

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:27.960
And so -- which it is.
And so we were like, yeah.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.196
And then Will Ferrell comes out
and it's like

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:31.998
he raps about birds.
-Boids.

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:34.734
And he goes like -- and I don't
know the words,

00:05:34.734 --> 00:05:35.902
but it's something like

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:39.205
[Humming]
♪ Is an amazing kind of boid ♪

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:40.739
♪ Boid, boid, boid, boids ♪

00:05:40.739 --> 00:05:42.308
♪ And that's a lot of boids ♪

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:45.110
-Yeah.
-And, like, obviously

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.280
it was a great sketch.
-Yeah.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.583
-And I do think, Seth -- and,
like, I don't remember this

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:53.953
or care, but I do remember once
you were going to kind of

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:55.387
rewrite it with us.

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:57.289
-I do remember. I said --

00:05:57.289 --> 00:06:01.260
I came out of that
on a weird boid high.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.528
And I remember saying
to you guys,

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:05.331
"I think I would like to
help you guys rework it."

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:09.301
-Yes, yes.
And then we kind of never --

00:06:09.301 --> 00:06:10.837
-I didn't do it, yeah.
-Heard back from you on that.

00:06:10.837 --> 00:06:12.037
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.037 --> 00:06:13.406
-I think what happened,
and don't take this

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:16.142
the wrong way, because
I actually cracked the knuckles

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:18.411
and sat down with "Boids" and
took a look at it.

00:06:18.411 --> 00:06:20.145
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-And I think it's a little bit

00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:22.849
like sometimes a doc is like,
"We can't save it."

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:25.217
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:27.520
But you would be willing to do
"Boids"

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.354
if we could get the original
gang back together?

00:06:29.354 --> 00:06:31.490
-It would be my dream.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.893
This show and doing "Boids"
are like my two dreams.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:36.428
-Talk about "Boids" real quick
like a QVC host.

00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:39.765
-Okay, so "Boids" is one of
those sketches that has it all.

00:06:39.765 --> 00:06:43.135
It has great music in it.
It has great jokes.

00:06:43.135 --> 00:06:45.471
It has great laughs.
And it's got great actors.

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:48.574
And honestly, if you watch
"Boids," then you're going to

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:51.710
go, "Wait a second.
I love sketch comedy."

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.480
And you're going to go,
"And I just adore it."

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.449
And don't we love it so much?
Because we do so very much.

00:06:57.449 --> 00:07:00.819
[ Laughter,
cheers and applause ]

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:03.055
-Vanessa Bayer, everybody!

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:05.991
"I Love That For You" begins
streaming April 29th

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:07.192
and premieres May 1st
on Showtime.

00:07:07.192 --> 00:07:09.695
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

